# QuoteIQ — DEPLOYED environment catalog (placeholders only; never commit a real value here).
#
# Laid out to match a `doppler secrets download --no-file --format env` export: alphabetical,
# KEY="value", so a real config can be diffed against this file line for line.
#
# SOURCE OF TRUTH: Doppler (project `qiq`). Each config syncs outward — one copy of every value,
# and neither Vercel nor GitHub is edited by hand:
#
#     Doppler config   ->  Vercel environment   ->  GitHub Actions environment
#     dev                  Preview                  dev
#     prd                  Production               Production
#
# The GitHub sync is what the `migrate` job in .github/workflows/ci.yml reads. It must target an
# ENVIRONMENT rather than repository secrets, or both branches resolve to the same project.
#
# TWO GROUPS OF VARIABLES LIVE IN DOPPLER BUT ARE NOT ASSIGNED BELOW, because this file may only
# assign keys the config schema actually reads (env-catalog-sync.test.ts enforces that):
#   * SUPABASE_ACCESS_TOKEN, SUPABASE_PROJECT_REF, SUPABASE_DATABASE_PASSWORD — consumed by the
#     Supabase CLI during `migrate`, never by the application. Note that the CLI reads the
#     password as SUPABASE_DB_PASSWORD; the workflow maps the name.
#   * DOPPLER_CONFIG, DOPPLER_ENVIRONMENT, DOPPLER_PROJECT — injected by Doppler itself.
#
# Adding a server variable means changing four things together: src/server/lib/config/schema.ts,
# this file, .env.local.example, and docs/environment-variables.md. The build fails if they drift.

# Pepper for the Q-19 first-party API-key hashing scheme (minimum 16 characters).
# WRITE-ONCE per environment: it is mixed into the stored key hashes, so changing it irreversibly
# invalidates every API key ever issued there. Treat rotation as a migration, not a config change.
API_KEY_PEPPER="<generated-api-key-pepper-min-16-chars>"

# Which tier this deployment is. Optional in the schema (it defaults to `local`), but that default
# is wrong for every deployed environment and silently so: `local` disables the guard in
# src/server/lib/storage/index.ts that refuses the in-memory fake storage adapter. Always set it.
# CI derives the same value from the branch and passes it to the seed as --env=; seed-target.ts
# refuses unless the two agree, so a config on the wrong tier fails loudly.
APP_ENV="<dev|preview|staging|production>"

# Bearer secrets guarding the cron and queue-drain endpoints (T-031). Generate per environment with
# `openssl rand -base64 32`. Never share one value across environments, and never reuse CRON_SECRET
# as INTERNAL_JOB_SECRET — they are separate so a leak of one does not authorize the other.
#
# EACH HAS A SECOND HOME: pg_cron calls these endpoints from inside the database and builds its
# Authorization header from public.job_cron_config (cron_secret, internal_job_secret), populated out
# of band per environment and never by a migration or the seed. Set here but not there means the
# schedules authenticate wrongly — or, when internal_job_secret is null, skip with a bare NOTICE.
CRON_SECRET="<generated-high-entropy-cron-secret>"
INTERNAL_JOB_SECRET="<generated-high-entropy-job-secret>"

# The origin pg_cron calls back on, read ONLY by `npm run db:cron:configure` when it writes the
# two secrets above into public.job_cron_config. Not a secret itself.
#
# MUST BE STABLE. A Vercel preview URL changes per deployment, so a schedule pinned to one stops
# resolving on the next deploy — use a branch alias or a custom domain. Origin only, no trailing
# slash and no path: invoke_cron_endpoint() appends `/api/cron/<job>` to it.
JOB_CRON_BASE_URL="https://<stable-origin-for-this-environment>"

# Password `npm run db:seed:demo` gives its personas. Read only by that script, never by the app.
#
# REQUIRED for any non-local target: the seed re-asserts every persona's password on each run, so
# without this it would fall back to the value committed in scripts/db/demo-data/catalog.ts and
# silently re-publish it — undoing any rotation. The seed refuses instead. Minimum 6 characters.
#
# The demo seed will not run against production at all, so this belongs only in environments that
# host demo data.
DEMO_SEED_PASSWORD="<generated-demo-persona-password>"

# Supabase Auth token verification (A-10, via auth.getClaims()). The server reads these rather than
# the VITE_-prefixed browser values below.
SUPABASE_ANON_KEY="<publishable-anon-key>"

# Deployed environments SPLIT these two, and the split is load-bearing (A-8):
#   SUPABASE_DATABASE_URL         Supavisor TRANSACTION mode (6543) — serverless request traffic,
#                                 which opens more short-lived connections than Postgres can hold.
#                                 A connection is handed back per statement, so no session state.
#   SUPABASE_DIRECT_DATABASE_URL  Supavisor SESSION mode (5432) — migrations, admin work and the
#                                 baseline seed. A dedicated session for the whole connection, so
#                                 multi-statement transactions, DDL and advisory locks behave as
#                                 they do on a direct connection. What A-8 forbids for admin work
#                                 is TRANSACTION pooling, not this.
#
# USE THE POOLER HOSTNAME FOR BOTH, not db.<project-ref>.supabase.co. Supabase's direct host
# resolves to IPv6 only (no A record) unless the paid IPv4 add-on is enabled, and GitHub-hosted
# runners are IPv4-only — so a direct-host value works from a laptop and then fails the seed step
# in CI with a connection error. The two differ only by port.
#
# Both carry the database password, so both are secrets.
SUPABASE_DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres.<project-ref>:<db-password>@aws-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:6543/postgres"
SUPABASE_DIRECT_DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres.<project-ref>:<db-password>@aws-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres"

# Bypasses RLS and drives the Auth Admin API (user provisioning, T-017). Server-only, always.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY="<service-role-key>"
SUPABASE_URL="https://<project-ref>.supabase.co"

# Browser-safe: compiled into the SPA bundle and readable by anyone using the site. Only ever the
# publishable anon key here — scripts/ci/scan-bundle.ts fails the build if a server secret or a
# server-only module reaches the built bundle.
VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY="<publishable-anon-key>"
VITE_SUPABASE_URL="https://<project-ref>.supabase.co"

# --- Optional server settings (defaults shown; assign only to override) ---
# LOG_LEVEL="<debug|info|warn|error>"          defaults to `info`
# NODE_ENV="<development|production>"          Vercel sets `production` on deployed builds
# STORAGE_ADAPTER="<supabase|fake>"            defaults to `supabase`; `fake` is refused outside local
# STORAGE_ATTACHMENTS_BUCKET="<bucket-name>"   defaults to `quote-attachments` (private, from migrations)

# --- Deliberately absent (documented so their absence reads as a decision, not an oversight) ---
# SUPABASE_JWT_SECRET  token verification uses asymmetric signing keys via auth.getClaims() (A-10),
#                      so the legacy shared secret has no consumer.
# ERROR_TRACKING_DSN   error tracking is disabled for MVP (Q-13).
